WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.995
In Rust, most values have one owner.

2
00:00:02.075 --> 00:00:05.015
When a value is assigned to another
variable or passed into a function,

3
00:00:05.095 --> 00:00:06.689
ownership may move to the new binding.

4
00:00:06.769 --> 00:00:10.157
After that move, the old binding is no
longer responsible for the value and cannot

5
00:00:10.237 --> 00:00:11.483
be used as if it still owned it.

6
00:00:11.563 --> 00:00:15.100
This rule prevents two parts of the
program from both believing they are responsible

7
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.127
for the same resource.

8
00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:18.051
Move semantics are common in everyday
Rust.

9
00:00:18.131 --> 00:00:21.618
Passing a `String` into a function that
takes ownership means the function now owns

10
00:00:21.698 --> 00:00:22.346
that `String`.

11
00:00:22.426 --> 00:00:24.868
Returning a value transfers ownership back
to the caller.

12
00:00:24.948 --> 00:00:28.535
Assigning one owned value to another
binding can make the original binding unusable.

13
00:00:28.615 --> 00:00:32.252
These behaviors make responsibility
explicit instead of leaving cleanup ambiguous.

14
00:00:32.332 --> 00:00:33.877
Some simple types implement `Copy`.

15
00:00:33.957 --> 00:00:37.345
These values can be duplicated implicitly
because copying them is predictable,

16
00:00:37.425 --> 00:00:40.365
inexpensive, and does not create confusing
cleanup responsibility.

17
00:00:40.445 --> 00:00:42.288
Integers and booleans are common examples.

18
00:00:42.368 --> 00:00:44.561
The important concept is not the exact
list of types,

19
00:00:44.641 --> 00:00:47.730
but the distinction between moving
ownership and copying simple values.

20
00:00:47.810 --> 00:00:49.703
`Clone` is different because it is
explicit.

21
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.727
Cloning may allocate memory, duplicate
data,

22
00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:53.849
or perform a deeper operation than a
simple copy.

23
00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:57.268
A clone can be exactly the right design
when two owners need independent values,

24
00:00:57.348 --> 00:01:00.836
but unnecessary cloning can hide unclear
ownership design and add avoidable cost.

25
00:01:00.916 --> 00:01:04.104
Reviewers should ask whether a clone
represents a real need or a workaround.

26
00:01:04.184 --> 00:01:05.829
`Drop` connects ownership to cleanup.

27
00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:07.204
When an owner goes out of scope,

28
00:01:07.284 --> 00:01:09.576
Rust can release resources associated with
the value.

29
00:01:09.656 --> 00:01:13.044
That resource might be memory, a file
handle, a network connection, a lock guard,

30
00:01:13.124 --> 00:01:14.669
or another cleanup responsibility.

31
00:01:14.749 --> 00:01:16.393
Understanding moves, copies, clones,

32
00:01:16.473 --> 00:01:19.214
and drops helps developers reason about
what exists, who owns it,

33
00:01:19.294 --> 00:01:20.340
and when cleanup occurs.
